::ENG2_U05_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Draft: "The library should stay open later. Students need it. It would be good." Feedback: "Explain what students need and use a formal tone." Which revision best uses the feedback?{
=A. The library should remain open later because students need a supervised place to complete assignments after school.#Correct. This revision adds a clear reason and uses a formal tone.
~B. The library is really good and stuff.#Incorrect. This is still vague and informal.
~C. Students need it because it is good.#Incorrect. This repeats vague wording.
~D. I like the library a lot.#Incorrect. This is personal and does not address the audience.
}

::ENG2_U05_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which feedback is most actionable?{
~A. Make it better.#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide revision.
=B. Add a specific reason explaining how extended library hours help students who ride late buses.#Correct. This tells the writer what to add and why.
~C. I do not like it.#Incorrect. This does not explain what to revise.
~D. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad and unhelpful.
}

::ENG2_U05_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision improves conventions?{
~A. Because students need a quiet place.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. Students need quiet, library open later.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=C. Because students need a quiet place, the library should remain open until late buses depart.#Correct. This is complete and correctly punctuated.
~D. Students need a quiet place, the library should stay open.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
}

::ENG2_U05_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which revision best improves expository explanation?{
~A. The library is helpful.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~B. The library helps people.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. It is good for students.#Incorrect. This is vague and uses unclear "it."
=D. The library provides a quiet workspace where students can complete assignments while waiting for transportation.#Correct. This explains the idea clearly.
}

::ENG2_U05_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which action best protects the main idea while revising?{
~A. Delete the whole paragraph and choose a new topic.#Incorrect. Revision should not automatically abandon the main idea.
=B. Keep the claim but improve evidence, tone, wording, and conventions based on feedback.#Correct. This keeps the main idea while improving audience fit.
~C. Ignore feedback from all readers.#Incorrect. Useful feedback should guide revision.
~D. Add unrelated details to make the paragraph longer.#Incorrect. Unrelated details weaken audience focus.
}
